<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toramana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 500</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Toramana was a ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Hunas</a>, or Alchon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, who invaded north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> around the late fifth and early sixth centuries CE. By around 500 CE the Hunas under Toramana and then his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihirakula">Mihirakula</a> occupied large parts of north-western India. These invasions contributed significantly to the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toramana.jpg" alt="Toramana" width="235" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toramana_portrait_and_initials_Tora.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Toramana, also called Toramana Shahi Jauvla, was king of the Alchon Huns from about 493. He consolidated Alchon power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and conquered parts of northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eran">Eran</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>. The Sanjeli inscription speaks of his control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, and his territory also took in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. Seals in the names of Toramana and Hunaraja found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kausambi">Kausambi</a> suggest that the Alchon Huns sacked the city around 497-500.</p><p>His inscriptions show him adopting Indian ways of rule. The Khura inscription from the Salt Range, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, gives him the Indian titles Rajadhiraja Maharaja beside Central Asian ones; written in hybrid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, it records the gift of a Buddhist monastery to the Mahisasaka school by Rotta Siddhavriddhi, who wished to share its merit with the king and his family. The Eran Boar inscription, cut in eight lines of Brahmi on the neck of a statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> as the boar Varaha, records a temple built by Dhanyaviṣṇu in the first year of the reign of the King of Kings Toramana.</p><p>A few of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins closely follow Gupta silver coins, with the king's head turned to the left, the fantailed peacock on the reverse and the name Toramana Deva.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Risthal inscription, found in 1983, records that the Aulikara king Prakashadharma of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> defeated him around 515. The historian Thaplyal argues that Toramana then ruled as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, probably continuing under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasodharman">Yasodharman</a>, and his inscriptions trace his change from an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharajadhiraja">Maharajadhiraja</a> to a subordinate king. He may also have been defeated by the Gupta emperor Bhanugupta in 510, though the inscription does not name the opponent in the great battle it mentions.</p><p>Toramana is known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajatarangini">Rajatarangini</a>, his coins and his inscriptions. The Gwalior inscription of his son and successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihirakula">Mihirakula</a> praises him as a ruler by whom the earth was governed with justice, and the 8th-century Jaina work <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuvalayamala">Kuvalayamala</a> says he lived at Pavvaiya on the bank of the Chandrabhaga and enjoyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Toramana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By around 500 CE, the Hunas under <b>Toramana</b> and subsequently Mihirakula had occupied large portions of northwestern and central India, and Mihirakula is remembered in Buddhist sources for his brutality towards monasteries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 500 <b>Toramana</b> invaded the Gupta Empire, reaching central India, as recorded at Eran.</blockquote><a href="/state/alchon-huns">Alchon Huns</a></li></ul></section>
